---
title: "Styling contract"
description: "Complete tested selector inventory and ownership rules."
---

# Styling contract

Import `svelte-advanced-cropper/style.css` once. Public visual components accept one `class` and a CSS `style` string. In application-scoped Svelte styles, use `:global(...)` for library-owned descendants. Theme entry points are described in [Themes](/docs/guides/themes).

## Ownership

A cropper's root class scopes its parts. SimpleHandler and SimpleLine put custom classes on the outer hit target; style their inner visuals through the simple-handler or simple-line classes. BoundingBox owns the `__lines` and `__handlers` groups and the handler placement wrappers. Use direct-child selectors when an outer box contains another cropper so outer styles do not reach inner controls.

```css
.avatar :global(.advanced-cropper-circle-stencil__overlay) {
	color: rgb(20 20 40 / 75%);
}
```

`--movable` and `--resizable` mean interaction is allowed; `--moving` and `--resizing` mean it is active. `--disabled` marks disabled hit targets or roots. `--hover` marks the control's hover state. `--visible` controls fading. Direction modifiers use kebab-case, while direction-map props use camelCase corner names such as `eastNorth`.

## Complete inventory

These expanded names are exercised by the class-inventory test in `src/tests/Customization.svelte.test.ts`. Internal canvas and transition elements remain styleable even though they are not public component exports. DraggableArea intentionally retains the `advanced-cropper-draggable-element` CSS name for theme compatibility.

### advanced-cropper

```text
.advanced-cropper
.advanced-cropper__boundary
.advanced-cropper__background-wrapper
.advanced-cropper__background
.advanced-cropper--disabled
```

